        <encodingDesc>
            <projectDesc>
               <p>The Welte-Mignon Advertisements Collection brings together print
                  advertisements for the Welte-Mignon reproducing piano that appeared
                  between 1905 and 1930. It was created as part of the PhD project
                  "Grünfelds Geist" at the University of Tübingen, which considers
                  piano rolls as objects of a digital edition. The collection is
                  published at https://anz.welte225.org.</p>
            </projectDesc>
            <editorialDecl>
               <correction>
                  <p>Errors of the original are not corrected; some are marked with
                     <gi>sic</gi>.</p>
               </correction>
               <normalization>
                  <p>Spelling and punctuation follow the original and are not
                     normalised. Text set in capitals is often transcribed in mixed
                     case and marked with the rendition <val>#uc</val>.</p>
               </normalization>
               <hyphenation eol="all">
                  <p>Hyphens at the end of a line are kept as printed.</p>
               </hyphenation>
               <segmentation>
                  <p>Every region of the original page is a <gi>div</gi> that points to
                     its zone in the <gi>facsimile</gi> with <att>facs</att>. The
                     lines of the original are marked with <gi>lb</gi>; the typography
                     is recorded with <att>rendition</att>.</p>
               </segmentation>
               <interpretation>
                  <p>Parts of an advertisement that concern other products than the
                     Welte-Mignon are not transcribed but marked with <gi>gap</gi>,
                     which describes them in a <gi>desc</gi>. Illustrations are
                     described in <gi>figure</gi>. Persons are linked to the GND with
                     <att>corresp</att>. Recurring motifs are marked as topics with
                     <att>ana</att> on <gi>seg</gi> and <gi>figure</gi>, pointing to
                     the taxonomy of topics; these are the editor's interpretation
                     (<att>resp</att>).</p>
               </interpretation>
               <stdVals>
                  <p>Dates of publication are given in ISO 8601 in <att>when</att>,
                     as precisely as they are known.</p>
               </stdVals>
            </editorialDecl>
